    WEEK 11 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>ALL CALLS STRAIGHT TO VOICEMAIL</h3>

  <p>
    “Dress for the job you want, not the job you have.” “You have to love yourself before someone else can love you.” “Stop buying avocado toast.” “Just drink more water to lose weight.” “Family is everything.” These clichés are annoying, aren’t they? When I am upset and hear “Look for the silver lining,” I get the same feeling as when someone is talking about self-care and they say to “take a bubble bath,” like we all have bubble bath soap in our bathrooms. Everyone’s got an opinion on self-care, and everyone’s got a contradiction fifteen minutes later. We’re told to “listen to our bodies,” yet we should “rise and grind.” There are more options for self-care than bubble baths and drinking tea, and self-care isn’t the opposite of productivity; it’s essential for maintaining the physical and emotional resources that allow us to function effectively and pursue long-term goals and success. Without adequate rest and recovery, constant productivity can lead to exhaustion and burnout, demonstrating that sometimes doing nothing is one of the most important things we can do for ourselves.
  </p>

  <p>
    What is self-care exactly? As previously stated, self-care is more than applying lotion and putting on a face mask. It can be:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Physical:</strong> Sleep, nutrition, exercise, rest, medical care</li>
    <li><strong>Psychological:</strong> Self-reflection, learning, mindfulness, boundaries</li>
    <li><strong>Emotional:</strong> Recognizing feelings, expressing emotions, self-compassion</li>
    <li><strong>Spiritual:</strong> Meditation, prayer, meaning, personal beliefs</li>
    <li><strong>Personal:</strong> Hobbies, identity, relationships, time for yourself</li>
    <li><strong>Professional:</strong> Work-life boundaries, breaks, realistic expectations</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    There are many ways to practice self-care. Going for a walk, a doctor's appointment, eating lunch at work, tending an altar, going to therapy, studying a language, calling a friend on the phone, painting, and journaling are all ways to practice self-care.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a difference between self-care and indulgence. Some forms of self-care are enjoyable. Others involve doing things we'd rather avoid, like going to a doctor's appointment, setting a boundary, or getting enough sleep instead of staying up all night. Practicing self-care is similar to maintaining a car: ignoring the check engine light doesn’t make the problem disappear, and turning up the radio doesn’t make the noise go away. Listening to our needs and being proactive about things like our health is part of self-care. The American Psychological Association’s article <em>The Ethical Imperative of Self-Care</em> discusses the importance of proactive self-care rather than waiting until stress has already become overwhelming.
  </p>

  <p>
    Self-care is individual. What works for one person may not work for another. A person working in retail who talks to people might need quiet time alone after a shift, but a person who works alone all day might need social interaction at the end of the day. Someone on their feet for their job needs a chair when they’re done; a person sitting at a desk needs to get up and go for a walk. The self-care needs of individuals vary. It’s not one-size-fits-all.
  </p>

  <p>
    Practicing self-care is something we do because we value our sanity and because we love ourselves. We prioritize ourselves and our mental well-being. We do it even if we don’t feel like it sometimes. Stopping to rest can be hard if there’s a long to-do list or the work is piling up. There can be a lot of fires to put out at work, for example, and the last thing a person might want to do is stop and rest. However, it’s imperative to keep our brains rested and fresh. If not, we can make mistakes if we’re frazzled. Productivity can become counterproductive.
  </p>

  <p>
    The World Health Organization identifies burnout as an occupational phenomenon resulting from chronic workplace stress that hasn’t been successfully managed. It identifies three characteristics:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exhaustion:</strong> Feeling depleted physically or emotionally.</li>
    <li><strong>Cynicism or mental distance:</strong> Becoming increasingly negative or detached from your work.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduced professional efficacy:</strong> Feeling less effective at what you do.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Clearly, a lack of self-care can have extremely detrimental effects in the workplace and in general. It can lead to reduced productivity, mistakes, and little energy. Taking time to rest can alleviate these things, and a fresh mind can produce good work.
  </p>

  <p>
    The productivity paradox is that we work harder because we want to accomplish more, but excessive demands without adequate recovery can eventually undermine our ability to accomplish anything effectively. We must ask ourselves: At what point does working harder stop producing better results? This varies from person to person. Once we experience the feelings above, it is time to rest and practice self-care. Sometimes, people wear exhaustion like a badge of honor because it “proves” they are working hard. This should not be true for you, reader. Someone working twelve hours will burn out at some point and produce sloppy work; compared to someone working fewer hours but staying focused, whose work will shine brighter? Unfortunately, our definition of success is often based on who is more dedicated to their work by spending more time on it and how much they burn themselves out for it. Thus, we treat ourselves poorly and sacrifice ourselves mentally and physically for it; is it worth the cost?
  </p>

  <p>
    Burnout is not totally on the individual. Workplace demands, supportive environments, and opportunities for rest and recovery matter too. This prevents the argument of “just take better care of yourself” from being thrown around. Environments matter too—whether they're toxic toward self-care or supportive of it. If it’s difficult to practice self-care in a workplace, then that is not the fault of the individual.
  </p>

  <p>
    A 2021 joint WHO and International Labour Organization analysis found that working 55 hours or more per week was associated with a 35% higher risk of stroke and a 17% higher risk of death from ischemic heart disease, compared with working 35–40 hours weekly. That's a striking illustration that overworking isn't merely uncomfortable; it can have serious health consequences. This is why sustainable recovery is necessary, and self-care is vital in the workplace. There are serious health consequences if it’s not practiced.
  </p>

  <p>
    Doing nothing. Sometimes one of the hardest things to do—or not do? Doing nothing isn’t necessarily the same thing as resting. If you are sitting on the couch worrying about tomorrow’s meeting, unfinished projects, or anything else, you are not resting. If you are mentally disconnecting from work during nonworking hours, you are practicing psychological detachment. Researchers found that psychological detachment was associated with lower fatigue, while having greater control over leisure time was associated with greater vigor. This means that having free time doesn’t necessarily mean we’re experiencing restorative recovery.
  </p>

  <p>
    But doing nothing makes us uncomfortable for some reason. We associate being busy with being successful. Time is money, is it not? And an unfinished to-do list doesn’t make us feel accomplished since the unticked boxes just get pushed to tomorrow—and tomorrow’s to-do list just gets longer. It’s difficult for people to just do nothing or relax when they have things to do and take time off from them. They feel like they are slacking or skipping out on things. Leisure is viewed as an achievement, and hobbies are seen as productive. We’re so obsessed with accomplishing things that we’ve started treating relaxation as another task to accomplish, like it’s a box to tick on the to-do list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Procrastination is something a lot of people say is a flaw in themselves. And they confuse it with rest. Procrastination means unnecessarily delaying something you intend to do, often despite expecting negative consequences. Rest means stepping away from demands to allow yourself time to recover. And doing nothing can be a deliberate choice to enjoy time without a particular purpose. They sometimes overlap, but there's no reason to assume someone who's doing nothing is being lazy. Procrastination is putting something off. Rest is taking a break and then getting back to it. There is a big difference between the two.
  </p>

  <p>
    Rest is also not a reward, something to be earned. It’s a necessity. It’s not a goal to be reached or a checkpoint. It’s something to be done when you feel it’s necessary. People say they will take a break when responsibilities disappear, but they never disappear. There will always be another email to answer, another load of laundry, another meeting to prep for, another appointment to go to. If rest is something we are only allowed to do when everything is done, when exactly are we supposed to rest? We need to listen to our minds and bodies and go rest when they are tired.
  </p>

  <p>
    A lot of people tend to think that productivity equals burnout, the most time spent working, and sacrificing their body, mind, and sanity for their work. This is not success. Success is taking care of yourself and practicing self-care. Someone can have a successful career yet have an unfulfilling life at the same time due to burnout and not practicing self-care. Success shouldn’t be measured by accomplishments alone, but by happiness, health, relationships, and personal satisfaction as well. The dangers of burnout include real health risks, and self-care can help reduce those risks, which is another reason not to measure success by accomplishments alone. Self-care is also not another obligation or item on a to-do list. It’s not a reward either. It’s a necessity for productivity, success, and good health. Doing nothing doesn’t need to accomplish anything either—it can just be for resting and decompressing. It can be just for enjoyment. Self-care is meant to improve our quality of life, so we should actually be allowed to enjoy life instead of constantly working to improve it. So settle in, turn your phone on silent, let the calls go to voicemail, put your feet up, make some tea, open your journal, and—hell—turn on the tap in the bathroom. Get the hot water running and take that bubble bath. Maybe it’s the pinnacle of self-care.
  </p>

